Sure 😊 let’s go step-by-step in very simple language so you can understand Referential Cursors in Oracle easily.

🧠 Step 1: What is a Cursor (Basic Idea)
When you run a SELECT query in Oracle, the result (rows) is stored temporarily in memory — this is called a cursor.

There are two types:

Implicit Cursor: Oracle automatically creates it for simple queries (like SELECT INTO).

Explicit Cursor: You create it manually to fetch multiple rows using PL/SQL.

🧩 Step 2: What is a Referential Cursor (also called Cursor Variable)
A Referential Cursor (or Cursor Variable) is a pointer that can point to different query results at different times.

👉 In simple words:

A referential cursor is like a TV remote — you can use the same remote to change channels (queries) and watch different shows (results).

It’s dynamic, unlike a normal explicit cursor which is fixed to one SQL query.

💡 Step 3: Why use Referential Cursors
They are useful when:

You want to return different query results from a single procedure.

You want to pass query results from one PL/SQL block or procedure to another.

You want to make your code more flexible and reusable.

🧱 Step 4: Types of Referential Cursors
Two types:

Strong Cursor: Has a fixed return type (structure known in advance).

Weak Cursor: Can return any type of query result (more flexible, but less strict).

Example:

TYPE strong_cursor IS REF CURSOR RETURN employees%ROWTYPE;  -- Strong
TYPE weak_cursor IS REF CURSOR;                             -- Weak
🧰 Step 5: How to Create and Use Referential Cursor
Let’s go step by step with a simple example 👇

Example: Weak Referential Cursor
We’ll fetch employees based on department id.

DECLARE
  -- Step 1: Declare a REF CURSOR type
  TYPE ref_cursor_type IS REF CURSOR;
  
  -- Step 2: Declare a variable of that type
  emp_cursor ref_cursor_type;
  
  -- Step 3: Variables to hold fetched data
  v_first_name employees.first_name%TYPE;
  v_salary employees.salary%TYPE;
  
BEGIN
  -- Step 4: Open cursor for a query dynamically
  OPEN emp_cursor FOR 
    SELECT first_name, salary FROM employees WHERE department_id = 10;
    
  -- Step 5: Fetch each record
  LOOP
    FETCH emp_cursor INTO v_first_name, v_salary;
    EXIT WHEN emp_cursor%NOTFOUND;
    
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Name: ' || v_first_name || ', Salary: ' || v_salary);
  END LOOP;
  
  -- Step 6: Close cursor
  CLOSE emp_cursor;
END;
/
🎯 Step 6: Referential Cursor with Procedure (Real-time Use)
You can also return a cursor from a procedure so another program can use it:

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE get_employees_by_dept(
  p_dept_id IN NUMBER,
  p_result OUT SYS_REFCURSOR
)
AS
BEGIN
  OPEN p_result FOR
  SELECT first_name, salary 
  FROM employees
  WHERE department_id = p_dept_id;
END;
/
👉 SYS_REFCURSOR is a built-in weak ref cursor type.

Call this procedure:
DECLARE
  emp_cursor SYS_REFCURSOR;
  v_name employees.first_name%TYPE;
  v_salary employees.salary%TYPE;
BEGIN
  -- Call the procedure
  get_employees_by_dept(10, emp_cursor);
  
  LOOP
    FETCH emp_cursor INTO v_name, v_salary;
    EXIT WHEN emp_cursor%NOTFOUND;
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Name: ' || v_name || ', Salary: ' || v_salary);
  END LOOP;
  
  CLOSE emp_cursor;
END;
/
