You’re absolutely right to think deeply about this — because at first glance, that example looks like it’s tied to the employees table, so it feels like a strong cursor.
But technically — it is a weak REF CURSOR, not a strong one.
Let’s prove this step-by-step 👇

🧩 Step 1: Definition Difference (Strong vs Weak)
Type	Definition	Return Type Fixed?
Strong REF CURSOR	Declared with RETURN clause	✅ Yes, fixed to one table/structure
Weak REF CURSOR	Declared without RETURN clause	❌ No, can point to any query result
🧱 Step 2: Look at the Code Again
TYPE ref_cursor_type IS REF CURSOR;  -- 👈 No RETURN clause here
emp_cursor ref_cursor_type;
Since there is no RETURN employees%ROWTYPE, this cursor is weak.

That means tomorrow you can reuse the same emp_cursor variable for any table or query, not just employees.

Let’s see this in action 👇

🧪 Step 3: Proof by Example — Reusing Same Cursor for Different Queries
DECLARE
  TYPE ref_cursor_type IS REF CURSOR;  -- 👈 Weak ref cursor
  c1 ref_cursor_type;

  v_name employees.first_name%TYPE;
  v_salary employees.salary%TYPE;
  v_dept_name departments.department_name%TYPE;
BEGIN
  -- First use: select from employees
  OPEN c1 FOR SELECT first_name, salary FROM employees WHERE department_id = 10;
  LOOP
    FETCH c1 INTO v_name, v_salary;
    EXIT WHEN c1%NOTFOUND;
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Emp: ' || v_name || ', Salary: ' || v_salary);
  END LOOP;
  CLOSE c1;

  -- Second use: select from departments (👈 same cursor, different structure)
  OPEN c1 FOR SELECT department_name FROM departments;
  LOOP
    FETCH c1 INTO v_dept_name;
    EXIT WHEN c1%NOTFOUND;
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Dept: ' || v_dept_name);
  END LOOP;
  CLOSE c1;
END;
/
✅ This works perfectly fine — because it’s a weak cursor.
If it were a strong cursor, Oracle would throw an error like:

PLS-00382: expression is of wrong type
when you try to open it for a query that returns a different structure.

🧠 Step 4: Example of a Strong Cursor (for comparison)
DECLARE
  TYPE strong_ref_cursor IS REF CURSOR RETURN employees%ROWTYPE;  -- 👈 Strong cursor
  c2 strong_ref_cursor;
  v_emp employees%ROWTYPE;
BEGIN
  OPEN c2 FOR SELECT * FROM employees; -- ✅ Works (same structure)
  FETCH c2 INTO v_emp;
  CLOSE c2;

  -- ❌ This would FAIL:
  -- OPEN c2 FOR SELECT * FROM departments;
END;
/
👉 This will throw an error if you try to open it for another table because the return type is fixed (employees%ROWTYPE).

✅ Step 5: Conclusion
Feature	Weak REF CURSOR	Strong REF CURSOR
Defined with RETURN clause	❌ No	✅ Yes
Can be used for any query	✅ Yes	❌ No
Structure fixed	❌ No	✅ Yes
Example type	TYPE c IS REF CURSOR;	TYPE c IS REF CURSOR RETURN employees%ROWTYPE;
✅ Therefore:
In your earlier example:

TYPE ref_cursor_type IS REF CURSOR;
it’s a weak ref cursor, even though the query happens to select from the employees table — because it can also select from any other table without changing the cursor type.

